Operating the Zabbix proxy on Windows
=====================================

This is the operator's side of build\win32\README_proxy, which covers building.
Here: installing, configuring, running, and reading what the proxy tells you
when something is wrong.

Every message quoted below is one the proxy actually emits. When you meet one,
searching this file for it is faster than reasoning about it.


What you get
------------

Active and passive proxy, SQLite3 buffer, Zabbix agent checks, simple checks,
trapper, preprocessing including JavaScript, data sender and housekeeper.

Not included: SNMP, IPMI, VMware, ODBC database monitor and ICMP ping. Those
checks are reported as unsupported rather than silently returning nothing.

The workers Zabbix forks on Unix are threads here, in one process. Practical
consequences: one entry in Task Manager rather than fifty, shared memory that is
ordinary heap, and a runtime control target that cannot be narrowed to a single
worker - see "Runtime control" below.


Requirements
------------

  * 64-bit Windows. Exercised on Windows 11, Windows Server 2022 and Windows
    Server 2025; Server 2019 is untested - see "What is not known yet".
  * Outbound TCP to the server on 10051 for an active proxy, or inbound on the
    proxy's ListenPort for a passive one.
  * Nothing else. The binary is statically linked; there is no runtime to
    install.


Installing
----------

With the installer, which is the supported path:

    msiexec /i zabbix_proxy-7.4.13-x64.msi SERVER=zabbix.example.com ^
        HOSTNAME=proxy-paris

  SERVER      the Zabbix server, "host" or "host:port". Default 127.0.0.1.
  HOSTNAME    the proxy name as declared on the server. Defaults to the
              computer name, which is rarely what the server expects.
  LISTENPORT  default 10051.

The installer writes zabbix_proxy.conf once, from those properties, registers
the service, and stops there. It does not start it: the server address and the
proxy name are guesses until you have read the file. Read it, then

    Start-Service 'Zabbix Proxy'

Startup type is automatic, so from the next boot it comes up on its own. Later
upgrades find the configuration present and leave it alone, so local edits
survive.

By hand, when you build from source or want several instances:

    zabbix_proxy.exe -c C:\path\zabbix_proxy.conf -i     install the service
    zabbix_proxy.exe -c C:\path\zabbix_proxy.conf -s     start it
    zabbix_proxy.exe -c C:\path\zabbix_proxy.conf -x     stop it
    zabbix_proxy.exe -c C:\path\zabbix_proxy.conf -d     remove it

    -S automatic|delayed|manual|disabled   startup type, default automatic

All four need an elevated prompt. `-i` records the configuration path in the
service command line, so moving the file afterwards breaks the service.

Neither route configures recovery actions, so Windows will not restart the
proxy after a crash. Both ways of asking the installer to do it are broken -
one through a custom action that fails the whole install, the other documented
by Microsoft as not working as expected - so it is left to one command, worth
running whichever way you installed:

    sc failure "Zabbix Proxy" reset= 86400 actions= restart/60000/restart/60000

That restarts the service twice, a minute apart, before giving up.


Where things live
-----------------

Installed by the MSI:

    %ProgramFiles%\Zabbix Proxy\
        zabbix_proxy.exe
        zabbix_proxy.conf              yours, left alone by an upgrade
        zabbix_proxy.conf.example      reference, refreshed by an upgrade

    %ProgramData%\Zabbix Proxy\
        zabbix_proxy.db                the buffer
        zabbix_proxy.log
        zabbix_proxy_*.sock            see SocketDir below

The data directory is deliberately outside Program Files: the service account
has to write there, and a service account writing into Program Files is a
mistake. Uninstalling removes the folder only if it is empty, so the buffer and
the log survive.


Configuring
-----------

The sample next to the binary documents every parameter. These are the ones
that decide whether the proxy works at all.

  Hostname          must match the proxy name on the server, exactly.
  ProxyMode         0 active, the proxy calls the server. 1 passive, the
                    server calls the proxy.
  Server            active mode: where to call. Passive mode: who may call.
  ListenPort        the proxy still listens in active mode, for the trapper
                    and for active agents.
  DBName            path to the SQLite buffer. It is created on first start.
  SocketDir         see below.

SocketDir deserves attention because it does more here than on Unix. Each
internal service publishes the port it listens on in a file there, which is how
`-R` finds a running proxy from a separate process. Two consequences:

  * the directory must be readable by whoever runs `-R` as well as by the
    account the service runs under, which rules out a per-user temporary
    directory. It defaults to %ProgramData%\Zabbix Proxy and is created if
    missing.

  * anyone who can read that directory can reach those endpoints, where a unix
    domain socket would have carried the directory's own permissions. Protect
    it as you would SocketDir on Unix.

Buffering, if you expect long outages:

    ProxyBufferMode=hybrid
    ProxyMemoryBufferSize=16M     minimum 128K
    ProxyMemoryBufferAge=600      minimum 600 seconds

Values stay in memory and move to the database when they age past
ProxyMemoryBufferAge or the memory fills. With the default `disk` mode every
value goes straight to SQLite, which is slower but survives anything.

Prefer the default unless you have measured a reason not to. Under a heavy
burst, `hybrid` has been seen to lose values: the two halves of the buffer
number their rows from separate counters, and when the memory half is holding
rows the database half has not seen, both hand out the same number. The insert
is refused and those rows are dropped, with this in the log:

    [Z3005] query failed: [0] UNIQUE constraint failed: proxy_history.id

The same burst in `disk` mode delivered every value. The counters are shared
code, so a Linux proxy loaded hard enough should be able to reach the same
state; it has not been reproduced there.

What a hard stop costs, whatever the mode: the trapper answers "processed" as
soon as a value reaches the history cache, which is memory. Killing the process
at that instant loses everything not yet written to the buffer. Measured, on
100 000 values acknowledged and the process killed immediately afterwards:
14 700 arrived. The same test against a Linux proxy 7.4.13 delivered 11 900, so
this is how Zabbix acknowledges rather than anything this build does. Stop the
service rather than killing it, and the values are flushed on the way out.

The buffer itself survives the kill. SQLite's rollback journal is replayed at
the next start, `pragma integrity_check` answers ok, and everything already
written is delivered - measured with the process killed in the middle of a
write, under load.


Encryption
----------

Both directions work, with a pre-shared key or with certificates, and the two
can coexist: the link to the server can use certificates while the hosts the
proxy polls use PSK. The host policy travels in the configuration the server
pushes, so it needs no local setting.

Pre-shared key, proxy side:

    TLSConnect=psk                (active proxy: how it calls the server)
    TLSAccept=psk                 (passive proxy: what it accepts)
    TLSPSKIdentity=PSK proxy-paris
    TLSPSKFile=C:\ProgramData\Zabbix Proxy\proxy.psk

Certificates:

    TLSConnect=cert
    TLSCAFile=...\ca.crt
    TLSCertFile=...\proxy.crt
    TLSKeyFile=...\proxy.key

The server side must agree. For a proxy object, tls_connect governs how the
server calls a passive proxy and tls_accept what it accepts from an active one;
tls_accept is a bitmask, so 5 accepts both cleartext and certificates during a
migration.


Runtime control
---------------

    zabbix_proxy.exe -c <config> -R config_cache_reload
    zabbix_proxy.exe -c <config> -R housekeeper_execute
    zabbix_proxy.exe -c <config> -R diaginfo=historycache
    zabbix_proxy.exe -c <config> -R log_level_increase

These reach the running proxy through SocketDir, so the config passed to `-R`
must be the one the proxy is running with.

The log level options reach every worker subscribed to the runtime control
service and say so for the rest. Targeting one worker by process id has no
meaning here: they are threads of a single process and no signal can single one
out. Measured on a proxy with 48 workers, raising the level reached 17 of them
- enough to diagnose, but not the per-process control Unix gives you.


When something is wrong
-----------------------

The log is the first place to look; at DebugLevel=3 a healthy proxy writes
almost nothing after start-up, so silence is good news.

  cannot start service [Zabbix Proxy]: [0x00000422]

    The service is disabled. Set its startup type: `-S automatic` at install
    time, or `sc config "Zabbix Proxy" start= auto`.

  "rtc" service is already running

    Another instance is using this SocketDir - often a proxy started from a
    console while the service is running. If nothing is running, the port files
    are stale and will be taken over automatically; a proxy that still refuses
    predates that behaviour and should be upgraded.

  listener failed: bind() for [[-]:10051] failed

    ListenPort is taken. Note that under WSL mirrored networking, a listener
    inside the distro occupies the port on the Windows side too.

  Unable to connect to [<server>]:10051 ... Could not to connect to server.

    The server is unreachable. The proxy retries indefinitely and keeps
    collecting into its buffer; this is not fatal and is expected at boot,
    when the proxy usually starts before whatever the server runs on.

  cannot send proxy data to server: connection of type "..." is not allowed

    The server refuses this proxy's encryption. Compare TLSConnect here with
    tls_accept on the proxy object there.

  connection of type "..." is not allowed for host "<host>"

    A host's own encryption policy, not the proxy's. It comes from the server
    configuration; change it there.

  Zabbix agent item "..." failed: first network error

    The agent is unreachable, or refuses this proxy's encryption. Reproduce it
    with zabbix_get from the proxy machine before suspecting the proxy.

  unknown parameter "..." in config file

    A parameter name that does not exist in this version. The proxy names it
    and refuses to start rather than ignoring it.

  ProxyMemoryBufferSize configuration parameter can be set only when
  ProxyBufferMode is set to "memory" or "hybrid"

    Validation reports every problem it finds, not just the first. Read the
    whole output.


Upgrading
---------

Run the new installer over the old one, with the proxy still running. It stops
the service, replaces the binary and the sample, and leaves your configuration
and the buffer alone.

It does not start the service again. Start it yourself, or the proxy is down
until someone notices:

    net start "Zabbix Proxy"

What an upgrade was measured to do, installing one package over another while
the service ran: the old entry is replaced rather than left beside the new one,
the version in Programs and Features is the new one, edits made to
zabbix_proxy.conf are still there, the buffer is the same file it was, and
zabbix_proxy.conf.example is refreshed. An uninstall then leaves the buffer and
your configuration behind, and takes the service.

The buffer is a database carrying a schema version, which the proxy checks at
start-up and reports:

    current database version (mandatory/optional): 07040000/07040011
    required mandatory version: 07040000

A buffer has been carried across binary replacements of the same version, an
operating system restart and a package upgrade, without loss. A change of
Zabbix version has not been tested either way. If a proxy refuses a buffer it
cannot read, removing zabbix_proxy.db lets it start again, at the cost of
whatever the buffer still held.


Uninstalling
------------

Through Settings, or:

    msiexec /x zabbix_proxy-7.4.13-x64.msi

The service is stopped and removed. The data directory stays if it still holds
the buffer or the log; delete it by hand when you are sure you no longer want
them.


What is not known yet
---------------------

Stated plainly, because a monitoring component that surprises you is worse than
one with documented limits.

  * Throughput has been measured only against one server, on one machine, with
    trapper items. Six senders delivering 960 000 values took 102 seconds to
    accept and about four minutes to relay in full. There is no figure for how
    many hosts one Windows proxy sustains, so size it conservatively and watch
    zabbix[wcache,values] and the buffer file.

  * ProxyBufferMode=hybrid loses values under a heavy burst; see Configuring.

  * Windows Server 2019 has not been exercised. Every check runs on Windows 11,
    on Windows Server 2022 Datacenter (10.0.20348) and on Windows Server 2025
    Datacenter (10.0.26100) - the self-test, the service registration and the
    installer, against the one binary rather than three builds of it. Server
    2019 is absent because its runner image is retired, so nothing here says
    anything about it either way.

  * The housekeeper has not run for days on end. Watch the size of
    zabbix_proxy.db over the first week.

  * The binary is unsigned unless you sign it yourself. SmartScreen warns on
    every install and some endpoint protection blocks it outright.

  * After a long server outage, collection pauses for around three minutes once
    the server returns, for every item at once and with nothing in the log. A
    Linux proxy of the same version does the same, so this is Zabbix behaviour
    rather than something this build introduced.
